course: dictionary
lesson: 13045
title: Far, farther, farthest
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Far tells us how long a distance is. When we compare distances, the word changes: far, then farther, then farthest. Farthest is for the greatest distance of all, when you compare three or more things.
explain: «Mia jumped 2 metres, Sam jumped 3 metres and Ravi jumped 4 metres.» Ravi jumped farthest, because his jump was the greatest distance of the three. Ask yourself: is this the most far away out of all of them?
explain: Farthest can also mean the very end of something, as in «the farthest point of the lake». It can also tell us how far something goes: «Who can throw farthest?» means who can throw to the greatest distance.
fact: Far, farther and farthest are the three steps of comparing distance: far, farther, farthest.
fact: «Farthest» means at the greatest distance, the most far away.
fact: «Farthest» is the top step of «far», used when you compare three or more things.
fact: «Farthest» is often used with «from», as in «Neptune is farthest from the Sun» out of all the planets.
exercise: «Mia threw the ball 5 metres, Ben threw it 8 metres and Zara threw it 12 metres.» Who threw the ball farthest? | Ben | *Zara | Mia
exercise: What does «farthest away» mean? | Very fast | *At the greatest distance away | Nearest | Lost and cannot be found
exercise: Which word is the top step of far: far, farther, ___? | *farthest | nearer | longer | closer
test: «The shop is near, the library is farther, and the park is farthest.» Which place is the farthest away? | *Park | Library | Shop
test: Ravi swam the ___ of the four friends. Which word fits? | far | nearest | *farthest | farther
exam: Four friends jumped: Lily 1 metre, Omar 2 metres, Tom 3 metres and Jess 4 metres. Who jumped farthest? | Tom | *Jess | Omar | Lily
